TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2): Space Mission Design","overview":"Develop a preliminary space-mission design that benefits Australia and report the mission concept and its space-segment elements. Use mission-design software of your choice, supported by hand calculations if useful, and justify the proposed design."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale. Include the following required information: What is the spacecraft's primary mission? Why is the mission needed? How many spacecraft are used and why? Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design. Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate. Include relevant screenshots and software files in the main body of the report and/or appendix. Include the following information: Where will it launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? Which ground control facilities will you use? How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (e.g. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Executive Summary; 1 Introduction"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"1 Introduction, Number of spacecraft"},{"id":"R3","met":"yes","where":"1 Introduction, Users"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"2.2 Launch"},{"id":"R5","met":"yes","where":"2.3 Orbit, Table 4"},{"id":"R6","met":"yes","where":"2.3 Orbit, Manoeuvres"},{"id":"R7","met":"yes","where":"2.4 Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R8","met":"yes","where":"2.5 Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","where":"2.3 Orbit, Figure 1 (STK 11)"},{"id":"R10","met":"partial","where":"Appendix A: STK files and settings"},{"id":"R11","met":"yes","where":"Bibliography"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"2.2 Launch","issue":"A retrograde orbit (i = 97.79°) requires a launch heading with a westward component relative to the eastward direction. Describing it as launched 'slightly west of north' with β = −7.8° is inconsistent and the azimuth convention is unclear."},{"where":"2.4 Ground Control & Communications","issue":"Contact-time fraction is inconsistent: 510 s of 771 s is about 66%, not 60%, and 6 passes × 510 s would give about 51 min, not the 34 min used in Table 6."},{"where":"Table 6: Daily data budget","issue":"Daily imaging assumes one 60 km × 1,000 km strip per day, which conflicts with about 14.9 orbits per day and six passes; the imaged area and raw data volume are not clearly derived from the orbit and access results."},{"where":"2.4 Ground Control & Communications","issue":"The downlink margin of 3.5 is computed without a link budget; the claim that all images reach the ground in a day is therefore unsupported."},{"where":"2.2 Launch","issue":"Claim that Mount Pleasant is at a 'similar latitude' commercial alternative and that Vega C launch-readiness timing is fixed are asserted without sourcing."}],"overall":"A strong, well-supported preliminary design covering all requirements with STK evidence, hand calculations and clear structure. Main weaknesses are a launch-azimuth convention error for a retrograde orbit, inconsistent contact-time and data-budget figures, and an unbudgeted X-band link. Overall this is a high Accomplished-level report with some technical inaccuracies.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Accomplished","mark":9.0,"scale":10.0,"points":9.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"All four required items (mission, why needed, number of spacecraft with justification, operator and customers) are addressed in detail, with literature cited for the bleaching problem and fluid-lensing context. Minor weakness: the rationale for using one satellite leans on assertion rather than quantified trade.","evidence":[{"where":"1 Introduction, Primary mission","quote":"The primary mission of ZYZZsat is to image the whole Great Barrier Reef in visible bands at 5 m resolution"},{"where":"1 Introduction, Users","quote":"The operator is the Australian Institute of Marine Science (AIMS), which already runs the long-term monitoring program for the reef"}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Accomplished","mark":8.0,"scale":10.0,"points":48.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"Covers every required element with hand calculations that largely check out (J2 RAAN rate, diffraction aperture, swath, drag decay), supported by STK figures, access tables and an appendix file list. Deductions for: retrograde launch azimuth described as 'west of north' which conflicts with a retrograde orbit; the 510/771 s (66%) versus 60% contact figure; an unbudgeted X-band link; and a one-strip-per-day imaging assumption that does not match six passes.","evidence":[{"where":"2.2 Launch","quote":"The launch azimuth β needed to reach the inclination i from latitude φ is found from sin β = cos i / cos φ"},{"where":"2.3 Orbit, Table 3","quote":"Days to reach any point of the reef | 2,698 km / 560 km | about 5 days"},{"where":"2.5 Mission Duration & EOL, Table 7","quote":"Sail deployed (4 m² sail plus body) | 4.3 m² | 5.8 m | 31 km per year | about 2.2 years"}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Accomplished","mark":8.0,"scale":10.0,"points":8.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"Conclusions are specific, tie back to quantified results (5-day revisit, 3.5x margin, 2-year decay) and identify concrete limitations with next steps. Some claims, such as the 'weekly coverage reef managers prefer', are not sourced.","evidence":[{"where":"3 Conclusion","quote":"The design has four limitations. First, Vega C has not yet flown, so the Falcon 9 rideshare service is kept as a fallback"}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Accomplished","mark":8.0,"scale":10.0,"points":16.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"Clear engineering structure with an executive summary, numbered and captioned tables and figures, and in-text numbered citations matching the 15-entry bibliography. Writing is clear. Minor inconsistency: the access report uses a 0° mask while the data budget uses 10°.","evidence":[{"where":"Contents and section structure","quote":"Executive Summary; 1 Introduction; 2 Mission Design; 3 Conclusion; Bibliography; Appendix A"},{"where":"2.4 Ground Control & Communications, Table 5","quote":"Table 5: STK access report, ZYZZsat to Mount Pleasant Radio Observatory (elevation above 0°)."}]}]}
